Here are some strategy invariants that seem especially reusable:

Animal	Strategy invariant	Where it transfers
Beaver	Change the environment so it does recurring work for you.	Fix a repeated bottleneck at its source: rearrange a workspace, automate a handoff, or make the desired action easier to start.
Slime mold	Explore several routes; reinforce the ones that keep producing value.	Try small, low-cost experiments before committing. Give successful paths more attention and let dead ends fade.
Honeybee	Let scouts search widely, then send back a compact signal others can act on.	Separate discovery from execution: many people or tools can look around, while the useful finding gets summarized into a clear next move.
Desert ant	Keep a rough sense of direction, then correct it against landmarks.	Track progress with both a goal and real checkpoints. If your internal sense of “we’re getting closer” drifts, compare it with evidence.
Squirrel	Store value in several places; some forgotten stores become future growth.	Keep promising ideas in small, searchable notes. An idea that doesn’t fit today may become useful when a new project gives it soil.
Ant colony	Reinforce active routes, but let unused trails fade.	Make current priorities visible without treating every old priority as permanent. Keep the history retrievable; let its pull on attention decay.
Sea otter	Use a tool to unlock food that would otherwise be inaccessible.	Find the small reusable instrument—a template, script, checklist, or relationship—that makes a difficult task repeatable.

A lucky combination: squirrel caches + slime-mold exploration + ant-trail decay could become an “idea orchard” for Pantheon. Save ideas as small nodes, occasionally pair one with something from a distant branch, test the most promising collision cheaply, and let useful connections gain prominence while stale ones lose attention.

That creates a practical way to get lucky: preserve more seeds than you can use today, keep exploring, and make it easy for an unexpected connection to sprout later.